+
+# Module 1: SMP/E Overview
+
+## Module goals
+
+After this module you can:
+
+- Explain what SMP/E is and the problem it solves.
+- Describe how a product, a SYSMOD, and an element relate.
+- Identify the SMP/E database data sets and what each holds.
+- Trace a SYSMOD through RECEIVE, APPLY, ACCEPT, RESTORE, and REJECT.
+- Choose between a single-CSI and multiple-CSI structure.
+- Invoke SMP/E from JCL and interpret the basic output.
+
+Course objectives covered: what SMP/E is and how it works, the installation process, configuration and control options (introduction), interpreting output (introduction).
+
+Conventions used in all examples: high-level qualifier `SMPE.LAB`, zones `GLOBAL`, `TGT1`, `DLB1`, SREL `Z038`, fictional product FMID `HXYZ110`. Replace `&VOL` and the job card with site values. Every code file used by this module is listed in `labs/manifest.json` with its verification status; the status is printed under each file below. A file marked `verified: no` has not been run on a z/OS system and must be validated against the current SMP/E User's Guide and Commands book before use.
+
+---
+
+## What is System Modification Program Extended?
+
+SMP/E (System Modification Program Extended) is the z/OS tool for installing software and maintenance and tracking what was installed. The program is `GIMSMP`. It does three jobs:
+
+1. Installs: it puts product code and service into the correct libraries using the correct utilities.
+2. Controls: it checks that a change is compatible with what is already installed (prerequisites, requisites, supersedes, holds).
+3. Tracks: it records every change in a database so that any element's level and origin can be determined and, within limits, backed out.
+
+Without SMP/E, a system programmer would have to know which library holds each module, which fix supersedes which, and which fix was installed on which date. SMP/E stores that knowledge.
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+    V[Vendor or IBM package] -->|SYSMODs and HOLDDATA| S[SMP/E]
+    S -->|installs| T[Target libraries - running system]
+    S -->|installs| D[Distribution libraries - backup copy]
+    S -->|records| C[(CSI and other SMP/E data sets)]
+    C -->|reports and queries| P[System programmer]
+```
+
+Analogy used by IBM: the CSI is a library card catalog, the target and distribution libraries are the shelves, and zones are the drawers of the catalog.
+
+SMP/E-managed software is installed in two copies on purpose:
+
+| Library type | Purpose | Tracked in |
+|---|---|---|
+| Target libraries | Used at run time by the system | Target zone |
+| Distribution libraries (DLIBs) | Pristine, tested copy used as the source for rebuilding and restoring | Distribution zone |
+
+---
+
+## What makes a product?
+
+SMP/E has no single "product" object that is mandatory. A product is a set of elements that SMP/E installs through one or more function SYSMODs, identified by their FMIDs (function modification identifiers).
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TD
+    P[Product - for example XYZ] --> F1[Base function FMID HXYZ110]
+    P --> F2[Dependent function FMID JXYZ111 - requires HXYZ110]
+    F1 --> E1[MOD elements]
+    F1 --> E2[MAC elements]
+    F1 --> E3[SRC elements]
+    F1 --> E4[DATA / HFS / other elements]
+    F2 --> E5[Elements for the optional feature]
+```
+
+Key terms:
+
+| Term | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| FMID | The function SYSMOD ID that introduced an element. Service SYSMODs name the FMID they apply to (`++VER ... FMID(...)`). |
+| Base function | A function SYSMOD that stands alone (for example a product's base code). |
+| Dependent function | A function SYSMOD that requires a base function and adds to or extends it. |
+| Product / feature entries | Optional descriptive entries (`++PRODUCT`, `++FEATURE`) that group FMIDs for reporting and ordering. Covered in Module 6. |
+| SREL | System release the code is built for (for example `Z038` for z/OS). Zones are tied to an SREL so SMP/E can reject mismatched software. |
+
+Practical consequences:
+
+- A fix is not installed against "the product" but against an FMID.
+- Uninstalling a product means deleting its function SYSMOD's elements; SMP/E does this through RESTORE of the function (target) and library cleanup, not a single "uninstall" command.
+
+---
+
+## SMP/E elements
+
+An element is the smallest unit SMP/E tracks and installs. SMP/E assigns each installed element a type.
+
+| Element type | MCS | Typical content | Typical library |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| MOD | `++MOD` | Object module | Distribution library (e.g. `AXYZMOD`), then link-edited into an LMOD in a target library |
+| LMOD | built from JCLIN | Load module produced by linking MODs | Target library (e.g. `SYS1.LINKLIB`) |
+| MAC | `++MAC` | Assembler macro | Macro library |
+| SRC | `++SRC` | Source (e.g. sample code) | Source library |
+| DATA | `++DATA` | Non-program data | Data library |
+| PROGRAM | `++PROGRAM` | Executable that is not link-edited by SMP/E | Program library |
+| HFS / zFS path elements | `++HFS`, `++JAR`, etc. | Files in the z/OS UNIX file system | UNIX path |
+| Updates | `++MACUPD`, `++SRCUPD`, `++ZAP` | Changes to existing MAC, SRC, MOD elements | Same as base element |
+
+A key relationship: MODs live in the distribution library as object code; LMODs exist only in the target. JCLIN (Module 3) tells SMP/E which MODs are link-edited into which LMOD and into which target library.
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+    M1[MOD XYZA] --> L[LMOD XYZLOAD]
+    M2[MOD XYZB] --> L
+    L --> TL[(Target library - e.g. SYS1.LINKLIB)]
+    M1 --> DL[(DLIB - AXYZMOD)]
+    M2 --> DL
+```
+
+---
+
+## How products are packaged
+
+Products and service arrive as SYSMODs, written as modification control statements (MCS) plus modification text. IBM packaging forms:
+
+| Form | Description |
+|---|---|
+| Inline | MCS statements followed immediately by the element text (typical for small PTFs and the labs here). |
+| RELFILE packaging | MCS in one file, element data in separate relative files (`RELFILE(n)`), typical for products and ServerPac. |
+| TXLIB / SMPTLIB | Text kept in a library; SMP/E copies it into the temporary library `SMPTLIB` at RECEIVE. |
+| Hold data | `++HOLD` statements shipped separately (HOLDDATA) with service. |
+
+How IBM delivers (details in Module 7):
+
+| Delivery | Content | Install method |
+|---|---|---|
+| ServerPac | Complete, pre-installed z/OS or subsystem with the SMP/E zones and installation jobs | Dialog-driven install (shipped with its own CSIs) |
+| CBPDO | Products and service, no pre-install | RECEIVE-APPLY-ACCEPT |
+| ESO | Single-product tape (Expanded Service Option) | RECEIVE-APPLY-ACCEPT |
+| RSU | Recommended Service Upgrade, a tested service level | RECEIVE-APPLY-ACCEPT |
+| Internet | `RECEIVE ORDER`, retrieved from IBM servers | RECEIVE-APPLY-ACCEPT |
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TD
+    A[SYSMOD package] --> B{Element text location}
+    B -->|inline in MCS| C[SMPPTFIN]
+    B -->|RELFILEs| D[SMPPTFIN + RELFILE data sets or tape files]
+    B -->|library| E[TXLIB data sets]
+    C --> R[RECEIVE]
+    D --> R
+    E --> R
+    H[HOLDDATA] --> R
+```
+
+Example of the shape of a small packaged function (inline text):
+
+```
+++FUNCTION(HXYZ110) .
+++VER(Z038) .
+++MAC(XYZMAC1) DISTLIB(AXYZMAC) SYSLIB(SXYZMAC) .
+         MACRO
+&NAME    XYZMAC1 &P1
+&NAME    DC    CL8'&P1'
+         MEND
+```
+
+---
+
+## System modifications
+
+A system modification is a SYSMOD. Each SYSMOD has a seven-character ID, which SMP/E also records as the RMID (replacement modification ID) on every element it installs or replaces.
+
+There are four SYSMOD types:
+
+| Type | MCS header | Purpose |
+|---|---|---|
+| FUNCTION | `++FUNCTION(fmid)` | Introduces a new product or feature (new elements) |
+| PTF | `++PTF(id)` | Preventive service; fixes a problem and is distributed to everyone |
+| APAR | `++APAR(id)` | Corrective service; a fix for one customer's problem, pending a PTF |
+| USERMOD | `++USERMOD(id)` | Site-specific customization of an element |
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TD
+    FN[FUNCTION - introduces elements] --> PTF[PTF - preventive fix]
+    FN --> APAR[APAR - corrective fix]
+    FN --> UM[USERMOD - local customization]
+    PTF -. may supersede .-> PTF2[Later PTF]
+    UM -. may need re-work after .-> PTF
+```
+
+All non-function SYSMODs depend on the function that introduced the element they change. That dependence is declared in the `++VER` statement:
+
+```
+++PTF(UA00001) .
+++VER(Z038) FMID(HXYZ110) PRE(UA00000) .
+```
+
+Relationships a SYSMOD can declare (full treatment in Module 5):
+
+| Relationship | Statement | Effect |
+|---|---|---|
+| Prerequisite | `PRE` | The named SYSMOD must be installed first or in the same run |
+| Requisite | `REQ` | The named SYSMOD must also be installed (any order) |
+| Conditional requisite | `++IF` | Requirement only applies if another FMID is installed |
+| Supersede | `SUP` | This SYSMOD replaces the named one |
+| Negative prerequisite | `NPRE` | The named SYSMOD must not be installed |
+| Hold | `++HOLD` | Special processing or known error; blocks install until resolved |
+
+---
+
+## SMP/E database
+
+The SMP/E database is a set of data sets, each with a defined role.
+
+| Data set | DD | Format | Contents |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| CSI | `SMPCSI` | VSAM KSDS | Zones and their entries: global, target, distribution |
+| PTS | `SMPPTS` | PDSE or PDS | Primary SYSMOD store: MCS and element text of received SYSMODs |
+| SCDS | `SMPSCDS` | PDS/PDSE | Save control data set: backup of target-zone entries made during APPLY, used by RESTORE |
+| MTS | `SMPMTS` | PDS/PDSE | Macro temporary store: copies of macros replaced or updated by APPLY |
+| STS | `SMPSTS` | PDS/PDSE | Source temporary store: copies of source elements installed by APPLY |
+| LTS | `SMPLTS` | PDS/PDSE | Load module temporary store: used for load modules that need relinking after ACCEPT-time changes |
+| LOG | `SMPLOG`, `SMPLOGA` | Sequential | Record of commands and updates |
+| SMPTLIB | `SMPTLIB` | PDS/PDSE | Temporary libraries holding RELFILE text from RECEIVE |
+| Work files | `SMPWRK1`-`SMPWRK6` | Temporary | Scratch space |
+| Reports/output | `SMPOUT`, `SMPRPT`, `SMPLIST`, `SMPSNAP`, `SMPDEBUG` | SYSOUT | Messages, reports, listings, dumps |
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TD
+    subgraph Database
+      CSI[(CSI - zones and entries)]
+      PTS[(PTS - SYSMOD text)]
+      SCDS[(SCDS - APPLY backup)]
+      MTS[(MTS / STS / LTS - temporary stores)]
+      LOG[(SMPLOG)]
+    end
+    RCV[RECEIVE] --> PTS
+    RCV --> CSI
+    APL[APPLY] --> SCDS
+    APL --> MTS
+    APL --> CSI
+    ACC[ACCEPT] --> CSI
+    ACC --> LOG
+```
+
+---
+
+## Receiving a SYSMOD
+
+RECEIVE stages a SYSMOD and its HOLDDATA into SMP/E's own data sets. It does not touch the target or distribution libraries.
+
+What RECEIVE does:
+
+1. Reads MCS (and RELFILEs) from `SMPPTFIN`, `SMPHOLD`, or a tape/data set.
+2. Stores the MCS and element text in the PTS (or `SMPTLIB` for RELFILE text).
+3. Creates a SYSMOD entry in the global zone with status RECEIVED.
+4. Creates HOLD entries from HOLDDATA.
+5. Optionally assigns a `SOURCEID` so a set of SYSMODs can be selected as a group later.
+
+Command example:
+
+```
+SET BDY(GLOBAL) .
+RECEIVE SELECT(HXYZ110) .
+```
+
+Check what was received:
+
+```
+SET BDY(GLOBAL) .
+LIST SYSMODS .
+```
+
+To remove a SYSMOD that is only RECEIVED: `REJECT`. Detailed RECEIVE operation is Module 2.
+
+---
+
+## Applying, accepting and restoring a SYSMOD
+
+```mermaid
+stateDiagram-v2
+    [*] --> RECEIVED: RECEIVE
+    RECEIVED --> APPLIED: APPLY
+    APPLIED --> RECEIVED: RESTORE
+    APPLIED --> ACCEPTED: ACCEPT
+    RECEIVED --> ACCEPTED: ACCEPT with BYPASS(APPLYCHECK)
+    RECEIVED --> [*]: REJECT
+    ACCEPTED --> [*]: no SMP/E undo
+```
+
+| Command | Zone | Libraries changed | Reversible |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `APPLY` | Target | Target libraries | Yes, with `RESTORE` (while not accepted) |
+| `ACCEPT` | Distribution | Distribution libraries | No SMP/E command reverses it |
+| `RESTORE` | Target | Target libraries | Returns elements to their distribution-zone level |
+| `REJECT` | Global | None (removes from PTS and global zone) | n/a |
+
+Standard sequence with check runs:
+
+```
+SET BDY(TGT1) .
+APPLY SELECT(UA00001) CHECK .
+APPLY SELECT(UA00001) .
+```
+
+Test in production, then:
+
+```
+SET BDY(DLB1) .
+ACCEPT SELECT(UA00001) CHECK .
+ACCEPT SELECT(UA00001) .
+```
+
+If the fix proves bad before ACCEPT:
+
+```
+SET BDY(TGT1) .
+RESTORE SELECT(UA00001) .
+```
+
+Why the two-step design: APPLY lets you verify the change in the running system while the distribution libraries and distribution zone still hold the known-good level. ACCEPT makes the new level the known-good baseline.
+
+---
+
+## System Generation
+
+System generation (SYSGEN) is the older MVS process of building a system from stage 1 and stage 2 jobs. SMP/E interacts with it in two ways:
+
+| Case | Handling |
+|---|---|
+| Products installed by SMP/E | Normal case; no SYSGEN involved |
+| Products installed by SYSGEN | Their elements are in target libraries but are not tracked unless defined to SMP/E; the SYSGEN process is replaced for most z/OS elements by SMP/E and installation jobs |
+
+For current z/OS, SYSGEN has been superseded by ServerPac/CBPDO plus SMP/E for the base operating system. The curriculum covers installing SYSGEN products and SMP/E products together in Module 6; at this stage the point to retain is that SMP/E is the single authority for what is in the target libraries, and anything installed outside SMP/E is invisible to its checks.
+
+Verify the specific current SYSGEN statement against the SMP/E User's Guide before teaching beyond this level.
+
+---
+
+## Consolidated Software Inventory
+
+The CSI (consolidated software inventory) is the VSAM key-sequenced data set (`SMPCSI`) that stores the zones. Each zone holds typed entries; each entry describes one object.
+
+| Zone | Contains |
+|---|---|
+| Global zone | Zone index, SYSMOD entries for received SYSMODs, HOLD entries, global OPTIONS entries, utility entries, DDDEFs for SMP/E's own data sets |
+| Target zone | SYSMOD entries for applied SYSMODs, element entries (MOD, LMOD, MAC, SRC, ...), DDDEFs for target libraries |
+| Distribution zone | SYSMOD entries for accepted SYSMODs, element entries for distribution libraries, DDDEFs for DLIBs |
+
+Zone limits (IBM): up to 32766 zones can be in one CSI data set.
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TD
+    G[Global zone - GLOBAL] -->|ZONEINDEX| T[Target zone - TGT1]
+    G -->|ZONEINDEX| D[Distribution zone - DLB1]
+    T -->|RELATED| D
+    D -->|RELATED| T
+    T --> TL[(Target libraries)]
+    D --> DL[(Distribution libraries)]
+```
+
+Zone definition entries:
+
+| Entry | Role |
+|---|---|
+| `GLOBALZONE` | Contains global processing options; indexes the other zones |
+| `TARGETZONE` | Defines a target zone and names its related distribution zone |
+| `DLIBZONE` | Defines a distribution zone |
+
